&lt;p class=MsoNormal style='margin-right:-.5in;text-align:justify'>&lt;span
style='font-size:12.0pt'> I. Description of Imperatival Intent&lt;/span>&lt;/p>
&lt;p class=MsoNormal style='margin-right:-.5in;text-align:justify'>&lt;span
style='font-size:12.0pt'>&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span>&lt;/p>
&lt;p class=MsoNormal style='margin-right:-.5in;text-align:justify'>&lt;span
style='font-size:12.0pt'>            Imperative forms can convey a range of
meanings; they have &lt;b>(1)&lt;/b> &lt;b>varied quality&lt;/b>. In language, authorial
intent does not bear a one-to-one relationship with linguistic forms. In
assessing a writer’s use of the imperative mood, we look beyond the form itself
and infer its force from the near and distant &lt;u>literary context&lt;/u>, the &lt;u>historical
context&lt;/u>, and &lt;u>the nature of the case&lt;/u>.&lt;/span>&lt;/p></description></item><item><title>ISSUES IN WOMENS STUDIES A Guided Study</title><link>https://christir.org/essays/topics/christian-doctrine/mankind-anthropology/womens-studies/issues-in-womens-studies-a-guided-study/</link><pubDate>Thu, 02 Apr 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://christir.org/essays/topics/christian-doctrine/mankind-anthropology/womens-studies/issues-in-womens-studies-a-guided-study/</guid><description>&lt;style>
