&lt;/style>
&lt;div class="word-content">
&lt;p class=MsoNormal style='margin-right:-.5in;text-align:justify'>&lt;span
style='font-size:12.0pt'>            &lt;u>Relevant contrasts&lt;/u>. The statements
in 1 Corinthians &lt;b>14&lt;/b>:34 appear in a context that contrasts “not speaking”
with insubordination and causing confusion. Earlier in 1 Corinthians Paul talks
about women praying and prophesying (&lt;b>11&lt;/b>:2-16). Evidently Paul’s meaning
in the expression “be silent” is in contrast to speaking in such a way that
creates disorder and implies a rebellious spirit. The English word “quiet” may
be a closer synonym to the term in the original language, because &lt;i>“quiet”&lt;/i>
does not necessarily mean absolutely silent so much as verbally and
behaviorally restrained.&lt;/span>&lt;/p></description></item><item><title>WOMENS STUDIES AND 'STANDARD SITUATION'</title><link>https://christir.org/essays/topics/christian-doctrine/mankind-anthropology/womens-studies/1-tim-2/womens-studies-and-standard-situation/</link><pubDate>Thu, 02 Apr 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://christir.org/essays/topics/christian-doctrine/mankind-anthropology/womens-studies/1-tim-2/womens-studies-and-standard-situation/</guid><description>&lt;style>
