 /* Page Definitions */
&lt;/style>
&lt;div class="word-content">
&lt;p class=MsoNormal style='margin-right:-.5in;text-align:justify'>&lt;span
style='font-size:12.0pt'>            One point in 1 Timothy &lt;b>2&lt;/b> has to do
with husband-wife &lt;i>vs&lt;/i>. man-woman as Paul’s intended reference. Connecting
the statements of 1 Timothy with (a) a husband-wife contrast rather than (b) a
man-woman contrast represents a crucial variable between instruction on marriage
roles &lt;i>vs&lt;/i>. gender roles. A third approach to the text has something of
the same effect but seems to be more straightforward and therefore less cumbersome:
(c) Paul is addressing “standard situation,” that is, married with children.&lt;/span>&lt;/p></description></item><item><title>WOMENS STUDIES NOTATIONS</title><link>https://christir.org/essays/topics/christian-doctrine/mankind-anthropology/womens-studies/womens-studies-notations/</link><pubDate>Thu, 02 Apr 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://christir.org/essays/topics/christian-doctrine/mankind-anthropology/womens-studies/womens-studies-notations/</guid><description>&lt;style>
